     1	# guard-config-census — the config-census leg, v3.1 codified (recreation + practice)
     2	
     3	_Built 2026-11-15 on HQ's charge (owed checks, 2026-11-15 ~08:02 EST), codifying candidate v3.1 from recreation Cycle-008 Wave-6's Condense (which extended Wave-5's v3, which extended v2). Before this build the census existed only as candidacy text inside RUN records and as builder-run greps whose blind spots the scoring lenses kept finding. This file is the leg's standing definition. It owns ONE defect class: reusability failures whose mechanism is **config incompleteness** — shaping decisions living outside CONFIG, or CONFIG keys that promise control they do not exert (reusability stuck across Waves 3–6 and binding in practice waves since). It does NOT own timing, fit (guard-measured-fit's class), or content failures._
     4	
     5	## What the leg checks (per item, on the final recreation.html)
     6	1. **All-literal scan.** Every literal outside the CONFIG block is counted and classified: hex/rgba colors · durations · geometry values · numeric constants and formula coefficients. Each constant is classified in print as **subject-shaping** (it shapes what the subject looks like or how it moves → must be a CONFIG key; standing outside = HIT) or **structural** (loop bounds, indices, mathematical identities → exempt, classification printed per constant; the sr/3, sr/6 misclassification of Wave 6 is the cautionary instance — ratios that scale the subject are subject-shaping). **Addendum landed 2026-11-16 (recreation Cycle 024 Condense — the A9.6 background-coefficient clause, discharging Cycle 023's named consumer):** coefficients that set a BACKGROUND layer's growth, opacity, size, or intensity AGAINST the subject (dust-cloud growth/opacity, plate glow, reserve-field density/intensity, backdrop scale) are **subject-shaping** whenever a scoring lens can price them against the subject — a background layer's coefficients are subject-shaping when the readability or reusability lens can price them, and they must be CONFIG keys or be classified HIT; classifying them "structural" because they sit in a background generator is the named miss. Forcing evidence: T140 (Cycle 023) — the builder's census classified the dust coefficients structural while the reusability lens priced them subject-shaping at 78, the item's minimum (filed return: `hidden_files/study/recreations/T140-repetition-chant/codex/lens-reusability.md`, SCORE: 78, seat-verified at Cycle 024's Condense); INSTRUCTION Amendment A9.6 resolved the classification in the instruction layer the same cycle, and this addendum lands the same rule in the guard's own classification text. **Clarification landed 2026-11-15 (recreation Cycle 013 Condense, on T75's reusability-lens miss):** constants inside scene/asset GENERATORS that scale or shape the generated subject (skyline/scene geometry, generator seeds' geometric coefficients) are subject-shaping, not structural, whenever changing them changes what the subject looks like; font-family stacks named in CSS are likewise subject-shaping (typography is a shaping decision) and must be CONFIG keys or be classified HIT — a census that passes either class as "structural" has missed its own class. **Addendum landed 2026-11-16 (recreation Cycle 021 Condense, under codex-condense REVISE — invariant-exemption bar):** the declared-invariant classification is barred for any literal that implements a dimension a CONFIG key promises — if a geom.* key names it, the literals constructing it are subject-shaping and must read from the key. Forcing evidence: T130 (Cycle 021) — the car silhouette path coordinates and wheel offsets were exempted as declared invariants while geom.carW/carH promised that control; reusability lens 68.
     7	2. **Application-site resolution.** Every CONFIG key is resolved to at least one application site in the code, **including nested and array-member keys** (`artworkTags[].level` class — Wave 6, T43). A key with no application site is a HIT (unapplied key = promised control that does not exist). **Addendum landed 2026-11-16 (recreation Cycle 021 Condense, under codex-condense REVISE — value-flow site validity):** an application site counts only where the key's value flows into the construction of the subject the key names (e.g. carW/carH → the car body's path/transform and wheel geometry). A key whose only occurrences are its declaration, storage, or a consumer other than its named subject (e.g. carW read only by a puff-midpoint computation) is UNAPPLIED = HIT. Forcing evidence: T130 (Cycle 021) — census HITs 0 with "geom.carW/carH → car scale declaration" while carH occurred only in its declaration and the car path/wheels were hardcoded; reusability lens 68.
     8	3. **CSS-variable setter check.** A CONFIG key consumed through a CSS variable whose setter never assigns it is a HIT (the `geom.pieSize` false-negative class, Wave 6 T38: a reference, not a literal — invisible to literal greps, owned here explicitly).
     9	
    10	## Output form (filed per item, pre-wave)
    11	`config-census.md` in the item's artifact directory: literal count by class with the per-constant classification, the key→site resolution table, the CSS-variable check, and a HIT list (empty = PASS for this leg). The wave's RUN cites the path + hit count per item. The leg's verdict is ADVISORY (owner's 2026-11-14 ruling): hits are cured before scoring where the builder can, or carried into the wave printed with their disposition. The FIRING is not advisory: a wave whose record lacks the census for any item prints INCOMPLETE.
    12	
    13	## Call point
    14	Pre-wave, per item, after the build and BEFORE the five-lens scoring wave (wired into recreation INSTRUCTION Amendment A6 and practice INSTRUCTION Amendment A-C008). Practice re-attempts fire the same leg on the new attempt's file; the census runs against the attempt's own CONFIG, never the prior attempt's.
    15	
    16	## Scope honesty
    17	This leg proves the recreation is re-drivable from its CONFIG — every shaping decision named, every named key actually applied. It does not prove the CONFIG values are well chosen, the swap is beautiful, or the timing is right; the scoring wave prices those.
    18	
    19	## Firing record
    20	- 2026-11-15: BUILT (this file). First firing owed: the next recreation wave + the next practice wave, unconditionally.
    21	- 2026-11-15 (recreation Cycle 013 Wave 11): FIRST FIRING ×5 items (with guard-measured-fit) — final HIT lists empty ×5 after pre-cure cures; one MISS recorded (T75: scene-generator constants + CSS font stacks passed as structural; the lens priced it) and cured by the classification clarification landed in §1 above the same Condense.
    22	- 2026-11-16 (recreation Cycle 021 Wave 18): firing ×6 items; one MISS recorded (T130: declaration accepted as application site + car literals exempted as declared invariants; reusability lens 68 priced it) and cured the same Condense by the §1 invariant-exemption bar + §2 value-flow site-validity addenda above, re-encoded under codex-condense REVISE (the drafted site-category cure was rejected as amendment-theatre — the operative test is what counts as a site).
     1	# guard-measured-fit — the measured-fit leg (recreation + practice)
     2	
     3	_Built 2026-11-15 on HQ's charge (owed checks, 2026-11-15 ~08:02 EST), from the candidate spec filed in recreation Cycle-007 Wave-5's Condense and applied ad hoc since. This file is the leg's standing definition; before this build the leg existed only as candidacy text inside RUN records (Waves 5–6) and as a builder-side instrument — never as a guard with a call point. It owns ONE defect class: readability failures whose mechanism is **fit asserted, not measured** (readability stuck/under-floor in Waves 2, 3, 4, 5 and again in Wave 10 — T54 density, T57 attribution typography). It does NOT own timing anchors, density judgment, or any content-level failure; those classes stay with their own guards._
     4	
     5	## What the leg checks (per item, on the final recreation.html)
     6	For EVERY text element that carries subject content (titles, labels, captions-in-scene, cards, marquees, counters):
     7	1. **A measurement row exists, with provenance.** The item's build record contains a fit table with one row per subject text element: element id · container (box/strip/line) · the INSTRUMENT (harness run or rendered-measurement tool that produced the row) · the fitted element + stage geometry · MEASURED ink bounds after fitting (glyph ink height and width, from post-fit measurement of the rendered geometry at the 360 px stage) · container bounds · margin on the binding side. A text element with no row, or a row missing its measurement provenance, is a HIT. *(Provenance form added 2026-11-18, recreation Cycle 035 Condense — Amendment A12's paired landing: T196 + T197's model rows.)*
     8	2. **Fit is measured, never formula-claimed.** A row whose bounds are derived from the fitting formula's own assertion (font size × assumed factor — a character-width × factor MODEL) rather than from a post-fit measurement of the rendered geometry is a HIT — the formula is the claim, not the evidence (founding instances: T20's glyph-height overstatement, ink at 76% of claimed cell pitch; T19's floored 11 px phrase in a box that could not hold it; T57's attribution lines). In a seat with no browser binary, the lawful measurements are (i) harness post-fit ink measurement of the built spans, or (ii) rendered measurement with a NAMED stand-in font, the row labeled PROXY with the font named (demonstrations: T195 harness post-fit · T190 PIL rendered measurement, Cycle 035).
     9	3. **Legibility floor.** Measured subject-text ink height below ~11 px at the 360 px stage width is a HIT, whatever the formula claimed.
    10	4. **Hierarchy is measured too.** Where the design claims an intensity/size hierarchy between neighbouring text elements, the rows must show the measured ratio; a hue-only hierarchy against near-equal measured sizes is a HIT (T20's neighbour-window failure).
    11	
    12	## Output form (filed per item, pre-wave)
    13	`fit-table.md` in the item's artifact directory: the full row set (each row carrying its instrument/provenance per check 1; stand-in rows labeled PROXY with the font named) + a HIT list (empty = PASS for this leg). The wave's RUN cites the table's path + hit count per item. The leg's verdict is ADVISORY (owner's 2026-11-14 ruling): hits are priced into the build before scoring where the builder can cure them; hits carried into the scoring wave are printed in the RUN with their disposition. What is NOT advisory is the firing: a wave whose record lacks the fit table for any item prints INCOMPLETE.
    14	
    15	## Call point
    16	Pre-wave, per item, after the build and BEFORE the five-lens scoring wave (wired into recreation INSTRUCTION Amendment A6 and practice INSTRUCTION Amendment A-C008 — the legs run before scoring, not after). Practice re-attempts fire the same leg on the new attempt's file.
    17	
    18	## Scope honesty
    19	This leg proves text fits its container at phone width, measured. It does not prove the text is the right text, the right density, or readable in motion — timing, density judgment, and content stay with the scoring wave and their own guards.
    20	
    21	## Firing record
    22	- 2026-11-15: BUILT (this file). First firing owed: the next recreation wave + the next practice wave, unconditionally.
     1	# Guards — recreation
     2	
     3	## The judgment layer's home (owner's order, 2026-11-14 — 100% form)
     4	Form: **In-job form.** This job's judging = the Codex scoring wave (its review type, defined in `codex-scoring-wave.md` in this directory) + the mechanical guards below + `jev-prewave.md`. The wave's execution may be shared tooling; the definitions are this job's own files.
     5	
     6	## The commands (mechanical guards — callable, demonstrated)
     7	1. Construction-purity grep — recreation.html must contain no reference to source media, frames, or tracing (pattern census over the file).
     8	   Demonstrated: 2026-11-13, T15 attempt 1 (zero hits; Codex purity lens independently returned 100).
     9	   Pattern precision (2026-11-17, Cycle 026 Condense — priced across T167 · T170 · T175 in one wave): an in-file fragment reference `url(#id)` (an SVG paint-server or clip definition inside the same file) is NOT a specimen reference; such hits are classified in the item's build record and never counted as purity hits. The refusal class is specimen media, source files, and tracing — the grep's specimen alternatives (`segment.mp4|source.mp4|<video|<img|1235931636`-form source ids) stay unmodified.
    10	2. Swap test (parameterization proof) — headless harness loads the recreation with a swapped CONFIG phrase; target geometry must regenerate for the new words (a traced copy cannot).
    11	   Demonstrated: 2026-11-13, T15 attempt 1, LOGIC level (node harness, stubbed canvas: 5,060 → 2,728 targets). Pixel-level render proof is NOT exercisable from this seat (no browser binary) — printed limitation, carried in scores.md; the comparison page is the render surface.
    12	3. Syntax + determinism check — `node --check` on inline scripts; seeded-RNG presence census.
    13	   Demonstrated: 2026-11-13, T15 attempt 1 (both scripts clean).
    14	4. Scores arithmetic — recompute the wave average + min from the filed lens returns before scores.md prints a verdict.
    15	   Demonstrated: 2026-11-13, T15 attempt 1 (68/82/100/64 → 78.5 recomputed at filing).
    16	5. Segment binding — segment.mp4's timestamps/duration/ffprobe facts recorded against the packet's technique window before any build starts.
    17	   Demonstrated: 2026-11-13, T15 attempt 1 (1:24–1:40 · 16.0 s · ffprobe-cited).
    18	6. **Measured-fit leg** — `guard-measured-fit.md` in this directory. Per item, pre-wave: every subject text element carries a MEASURED fit row (post-fit ink bounds vs container at the 360 px stage, legibility floor ~11 px); a formula-claimed fit or a missing row is a HIT. Owns the "fit asserted, not measured" readability class (Waves 2–5, 10). **Built 2026-11-15 (HQ owed-checks charge); fires unconditionally at the pre-wave step from the next cycle; a wave record lacking a fit table for any item prints INCOMPLETE.** Verdict advisory; firing is not.
    19	7. **Config-census leg (v3.1 codified)** — `guard-config-census.md` in this directory. Per item, pre-wave: all-literal scan with per-constant subject-shaping/structural classification + CONFIG key→application-site resolution (nested/array keys included) + CSS-variable setter check; unapplied keys and unset-variable references are HITs. Owns the config-incompleteness reusability class. **Built 2026-11-15 (HQ owed-checks charge); fires unconditionally at the pre-wave step from the next cycle; a wave record lacking a census for any item prints INCOMPLETE.** Verdict advisory; firing is not.
    20	
    21	## Jev call definitions
    22	- `jev-prewave.md` — judges a build record's readiness to spend a scoring wave. **Firing: this call fires at its step on every cycle. A cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE.** (Designed 2026-11-14; attempt 1 predates it and ran under the §5 panel, which runs alongside.)
    23	- `jev-observe-exit.md` — the Observe→Plan gate for a recreation wave (queue state priced · prior/family scores read · mechanics seen · variables extracted · flow-field model + Owner Cures 1–2 carried in working memory · consumer named). Call point: INSTRUCTION §1 gate out. **Firing: this call fires at its step on every cycle. A cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE.** (Phase-guard augmentation, 2026-11-14.)
    24	- `jev-plan-exit.md` — the Plan→Execute gate (parameter table · Execute + Verify checklists as instruments · failure points priced · plateau floor rule priced in Plan). Call point: INSTRUCTION §2 gate out. **Firing: this call fires at its step on every cycle. A cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE.** (2026-11-14.)
    25	- Deliverable Jev: **none separate, by design** — the deliverable class (a scored recreation attempt) is judged by the five-lens Codex scoring wave + §5.5 consolidation; a jev-verify file would duplicate the wave. jev-prewave is its pre-wave gate. (Printed in INSTRUCTION §5 step 3b.)
    26	
    27	## Codex review types
    28	- `codex-scoring-wave.md` — the five-lens scoring wave (motion fidelity · timing/tempo · construction purity · reusability · readability). Demonstrated: 2026-11-13, T15 attempt 1 (four lenses then; readability added 2026-11-14 under the owner's order — the five-lens form demonstrates at the next wave).
    29	- `codex-observe.md` — Observe-phase source-record audit (packet-section completeness · prior-attempt record audit · segment facts). Call point: INSTRUCTION §1 step 1, per item. **Firing: this call fires at its step on every cycle. A cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE.** (2026-11-14.)
    30	- `codex-plan-review.md` — Plan attack priced against this job's recorded failure modes (convergent freeze · owner's two catches · parameterization reality · bar arithmetic · §8 size discipline). Call point: INSTRUCTION §2 gate out, alongside jev-plan-exit. **Firing: this call fires at its step on every cycle. A cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE.** (2026-11-14.)
    31	- `codex-condense.md` — Condense review: amendment faithfulness + four-layer coverage attack. Call point: INSTRUCTION §9, at Condense close. Consumer: the job's next cycle. **Firing: this call fires at its step on every cycle. A cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE.** (2026-11-14.)
    32	
